WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:01.934
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:04.400
is an Emmy and Golden Globe
winning actress.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.667
You know her work in the
critically acclaimed series

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.267
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:09.367
She stars in...

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.767
...which is playing at the
BAM Harvey Theater in Brooklyn.

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:15.434
Please welcome back to the show
our friend

00:00:15.434 --> 00:00:16.734
Rachel Brosnahan, everybody.

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:18.334
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:21.434
[ Band plays mellow tune ]

00:00:21.434 --> 00:00:31.434
♪♪

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:34.367
♪♪

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.934
Welcome back to the show!

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:37.400
-Thank you.
It's nice to be back.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.934
-I'm so happy to have you.
It is very --

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:41.601
I don't know if it's as funny
to you as it is to me.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:44.033
You finished wrapping
"Mrs. Maisel."

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:45.501
Obviously, you've done
this incredible show

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:46.767
for five seasons.

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:49.300
It takes place in New York City
in the 1960s.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.801
And now you've moved
on from that

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:53.734
to do this wonderful play

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:57.167
where you play a woman
in 1960s New York City again.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:57.834
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:00:57.834 --> 00:00:59.067
-So this is kind of
your move now.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:01.033
-I guess it is.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.801
Maybe next, I'll get to go
to the '70s.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.501
That would be really exciting.
-Oh, my God, yeah.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:05.901
We would love to see you
in the '70s.

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:07.601
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you so much.
Or the '90s.

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:09.834
That's vintage now.
Take me to the '90s.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.200
-Don't tell me
that the '90s is vintage.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.467
-Isn't that terrifying?
-That breaks my heart, yeah.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.467
[ Laughter ]
-Me too.

00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:16.801
Yeah, no. I mean, what's
interesting about this is

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:18.701
I feel like, you know,
obviously, it's a coincidence.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.634
I feel like I'm searching
for great parts

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:22.234
and great writing
and great scripts,

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:24.467
but, um --

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.801
I feel like Iris, who I play in

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:28.000
"The Sign in Sidney Brustein's
Window,"

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.067
would be one of the beatniks

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.767
in the comedy club
of one of Midge's shows.

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:34.300
-Yes.
-They're pretty different gals.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.200
-Exactly.
It turns out there were a lot

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.300
of different kinds of people
in New York City in the '60s.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.968
[ Laughter ]
-It turns out not all women
were the same.

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:41.801
-Not all women were the same.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.701
Despite men's best efforts
in the '60s.

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:45.267
[ Laughter ]
-That's exactly right.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:47.901
They managed to have
a breadth of experiences.

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:50.968
So, this is the same playwright
who wrote "Raisin in the Sun"

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:54.467
-Yeah.
-and this is her final play
and it's lesser known.

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:57.400
Is it cool to sort of bring back
a very famous playwright's

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.100
sort of -- you know,
I wouldn't say secondary work,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.601
but certainly lesser known work.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:02.534
Is it cool to bring it out

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:03.868
to an audience
that hasn't seen it?

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:06.667
-Yeah, it's been really,
really special.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:10.167
Lorraine Hansberry
was such a gifted writer.

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.634
So many people know her work
from "A Raisin in the Sun,"

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:13.834
but this is a really
different play

00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:15.501
about a really different group
of people

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.868
and it's a piece that is
so much lesser known.

00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:21.267
It's been really, really special
to get to bring it to life

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:23.667
with this cast.
Our director, Annie Kauffman,

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:26.567
did the play back in 2016
in Chicago

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:28.367
and it's just been the best.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:30.400
It's been so awesome to hear
from so many people

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.968
who were familiar
with her work from "Raisin"

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:34.834
about how this play
has moved them,

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.534
and also people who didn't know
her work at all

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:38.367
and this being
their first introduction

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:41.467
to the force that was
Lorraine Hansberry.

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:44.501
-You mentioned the cast.
Your costar Oscar Isaacs,

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:47.601
one of our favorites here
as well.

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:50.100
Oscar Isaac -- excuse me.
And how is it working with him?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.534
Were you familiar with his work?

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:54.501
-[ Laughs ]
Yes, yes.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:58.934
[ Laughter ]
No. Oscar's the best.
We've had so much fun.

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:02.734
I had, you know, I was familiar
with quite a bit of his work,

00:03:02.734 --> 00:03:04.367
but I did bust myself
the other day.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.968
He was messing around,
doing a doing a British accent

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.167
during rehearsals
and I was like,

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:11.334
"Oh, you know, your British
accent's pretty good.

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:12.934
Where did you learn that?"
And he was like,

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:14.267
"I -- I just --

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.200
you know, I just did it
in 'Moon Knight.'"

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.300
I was like,
"Which I have definitely seen

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.834
[ Laughter ]
and enjoyed very much."

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:24.801
Yeah. I keep getting busted --

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:26.000
-"You were so good in that,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.434
I thought it was someone else."

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:29.367
[ Laughter ]
-"I didn't even recognize you.

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.334
I thought that was
a British man."

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:32.267
-"That was you!

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:35.234
That was you."
-"It was you?!"

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.901
-You also have a very talented
young actor in the show,

00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:41.434
Julian De Niro,
who is Robert De Niro's son.

00:03:41.434 --> 00:03:43.200
-Yes.
-Does that mean
that Robert De Niro

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.667
has come to see the show?

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.801
-He has.
He's been an amazing dad.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:50.400
He's come to see the show twice
and he's so lovely.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.767
Julian is
extraordinarily talented

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:55.834
and has just been fantastic
to work with.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:58.067
But he did come
to our first preview,

00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:01.834
which is an amazing like
A++ dad move.

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:04.734
So mean, as an actor.
-Yeah. His first preview.

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:06.501
[ Laughter ]
-Actor to actor --
-You definitely want people

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:08.901
to come at least on night three.
-That's what I'm saying.

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:12.200
-Yeah.
-But he was very, very lovely.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.467
-When did you --
Were you doing school plays

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.534
when you were young?
Is that how you got started?

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:18.400
-Yeah, I did, you know,
I did some

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:19.834
kindergarten tree work

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:20.501
[ Laughter ]
that was --
-Oh, look at you.

00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:23.033
that was highly excellent...
-You still hear about it.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:25.467
-...if I do say so myself.
-Yeah.

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:27.167
-Yeah, I started, I started,
you know,

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.167
doing -- diddling around in --
That has a --

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:35.400
Just go back right now.
-That's like a stage term.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.234
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:39.067
Doing some diddling --
-To diddle is to sort of, yeah.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:41.100
-Thank you so much.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.734
In elementary school,
junior high, and high school.

00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:47.934
My high school had this really
cool program called Stunts

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:50.634
where the students
put on their own show

00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:55.067
and kind of rewrite the words
to well-known music and songs

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:57.667
and, as you can expect, it's --

00:04:57.667 --> 00:04:59.634
-So there's no sort of --

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:01.501
no overseeing adult?
-There's not a lot of adult

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:02.534
oversight, yeah.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.634
That's exciting.
-So, it's a [bleep]storm.

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:05.734
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:08.534
-But also kind of
amazing and inspiring.
-Yeah, or course.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:12.667
-I had maybe my most
traumatizing audition ever

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:13.801
auditioning for Stunts

00:05:13.801 --> 00:05:17.434
when I was a freshman
in high school.

00:05:17.434 --> 00:05:20.033
You know, I don't know about --

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.534
Can you sing?
-No.

00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:22.567
-You can't sing, not at all?
-I cannot.

00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:23.467
Not at all.
-Not even a little bit?

00:05:23.467 --> 00:05:26.701
Not -- I would say, based
on the feedback I received...

00:05:26.701 --> 00:05:28.267
[ Laughter ]
-Not at all?

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.901
-Yeah. It wasn't like,
"Go get lessons."

00:05:30.901 --> 00:05:32.634
It was like, "Don't do that."

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.634
[ Laughter ]
-From one singular person?

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:36.667
-No, it was like a group.
I got a lot of --

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:39.400
-It was an intervention?
-I had a weird thing happen,
which is my entire all like --

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.667
So, when I was growing up,

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:43.767
my brother and my parents
would say, "Stop singing."

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.701
[ Laughter ]
-Oh.
-And then I met my wife

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:47.367
and she would say,
"Stop singing."
-Oh, boy.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.968
-And now my kids are telling me
to stop singing

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:51.534
[ Laughter ]
and I'm like,
"What a coincidence

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:54.834
that not one of you
has an ear for talent."

00:05:54.834 --> 00:05:56.501
[ Laughter ]
-That's exactly right.

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:58.501
I want to see you
in the next Broadway musical.

00:05:58.501 --> 00:05:59.801
-Oh, my God. That's great.
-It's your turn.

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:01.834
-Come on opening night
because it's going to close.

00:06:01.834 --> 00:06:03.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.434 --> 00:06:04.901
Pull a De Niro.

00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:07.701
[ Laughter ]
Come come early and leave early.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.834
-It's a deal.
-So, what about you?

00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:10.968
You're not a singer, either?

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:12.801
-I feel like I'm the kind
of singer

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:14.667
who can fool you to an extent.
-Okay.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.367
-You know, I've taken
some voice lessons.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:17.834
I can get away with it and then

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.734
I better get cut off
or leave the room.

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:21.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:24.834
You know, I had done a little
audition for some of my peers

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:28.000
and then got a callback,
which was very exciting.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.434
And they handed me this sheet
music for this

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:33.200
slightly lesser known musical
at the time called "Wicked"

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.367
[ Laughter ]
and the song was
"Defying Gravity"

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:36.501
and they were like,
"Here you go,"

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:39.200
and I was like, "Oh, boy!"

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.367
And went up and did
an absolutely horrifying

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:43.634
rendition of "Defying Gravity,"

00:06:43.634 --> 00:06:46.334
where I missed my entrance
about five different times.

00:06:46.334 --> 00:06:48.167
And they kind of
just politely nodded me

00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:49.567
off the stage after a while.
-Yeah.

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:52.100
[ Laughter ]
And, in this play,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.734
if anyone comes to see it,
my character, Iris,

00:06:55.734 --> 00:06:58.334
gives a speech about auditioning
and how traumatizing it is

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:01.801
and I may or may not be drawing
slightly from that experience.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:03.267
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.267 --> 00:07:05.100
Not to, you know,
throw shade on Stunts,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.200
but it does seem like,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.501
if an adult was there,
they'd be like,

00:07:07.501 --> 00:07:10.067
"You can't make them do
'Defying Gravity.'"

00:07:10.067 --> 00:07:12.133
[ Laughter ]
-I'd like to hope
that they would've saved me

00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:14.834
from that drama.
-Yeah, well, look, obviously,

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:17.133
that trauma side,
it's all worked out really well.

00:07:17.133 --> 00:07:18.834
Congratulations on the show
and thank you so much

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:20.701
for being here. It is always
so lovely to see you.

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:23.234
[ Cheering and applause ]
You guys, that's
Rachel Brosnahan.

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:25.400
"The Sign in Sidney Brisbane's
Window" is playing

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:26.934
at the BAM Harvey Theater
in Brooklyn.

00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:28.467
We'll be right back
with Matthew Rhys.

